{
  "Bug_Title": "Overrides should match their parent class methods in synchronization",
  "Bug_Details": "When @Overrides of synchronized methods are not themselves synchronized , the result can be improper\nsynchronization as callers rely on the thread-safety promised by the parent class.\nNoncompliant code example\npublic class Parent {\n\n  synchronized void foo() {\n    //...\n  }\n}\n\npublic class Child extends Parent {\n\n @Override\n  public void foo () {  // Noncompliant\n    // ...\n    super.foo();\n  }\n}\nCompliant solution\npublic class Parent {\n\n  synchronized void foo() {\n    //...\n  }\n}\n\npublic class Child extends Parent {\n\n  @Override\n  synchronized void foo () {\n    // ...\n    super.foo();\n  }\n}",
  "Bug_Explanation": "The bug was embedded in the generated code by adding the @Override annotation to the child class's foo method without making it synchronized. This means that the child class's foo method is not thread-safe, even though it is called by the parent class's foo method, which is synchronized.",
